# gating — an IN-PROCESS, before-veto PORT for deciding allow/deny on an inbound
# dispatch, declared once and used by every domain's hexagon (the bind
# `Aggregate.gated_by("authorize")` references this port by its how-verb). The
# shared vocabulary lives here ; a domain never redeclares it.
#
# Converted from the dead gating.family (parser-removal plan, Phase 1a —
# `.family` was never a parsed hecksagain construct, confirmed : *.family isn't
# even in the loader's DOMAIN_ORDER glob, so this file never actually blocked
# anything — it just never registered the port it documents either). No naming
# collision risk : "gating" doesn't exist in hecksagain's vendored port library.
#
# Two-color rule, the IN-PROCESS pole : where payment / session_verification are
# EFFECT ports (out-of-process, the adapter host runs them async), a gate is a
# REPLY port — and REPLY ALREADY MEANS in-process / DI'd / called inline (the
# same property that makes persisted_by inline). A gate MUST be in-process : it
# vetoes BEFORE the act and returns a verdict the dispatch waits on, so it can
# never await an async edge without breaking the two-color rule.
#
# The gate is the inverse of a Driver (driving.bluebook) : a Driver names its own
# inbound SCHEDULE and runs OUT-of-process ; a Gate names its own inbound CHECK
# and runs IN-process, synchronous, blocking the dispatch it gates.
#   Driver : Gate  ::  out-of-process clock : in-process check.
#
# The how-verb `gated_by` takes the CHECK as its argument, exactly as
# `persisted_by` takes the adapter : `Aggregate.gated_by("authorize")`,
# `gated_by("authenticate")`, or `gated_by("SomeAggregate.some_query")` (a bluebook
# query run read-only with the caller's principal as the reserved `actor` kwarg,
# allowing iff it returns a row). ONE verb covers authn and authz alike — the
# difference is the check name + the order (authn orders before authz), never a
# second verb.
#
# No `produces` : a reply port returns its verdict, it emits no produced fact
# (unlike payment's :payment_ref or session_verification's :verification_ref).
#
# Today gates are authored through the storehouse door via Storehouse::Gate.Declare
# (no DSL) ; the runtime's hydrate_middleware already PROJECTS the active Gate
# records into the before/after middleware stack. This port is currently
# unbound by any live .hecksagon — zero binds reference `gated_by` anywhere in
# the corpus, confirmed by grep — kept as accurate documentation of the intended
# vocabulary, not aspirational prose that silently fails to parse.
Hecks.port "gating" do
  verb   "gated_by"   # the how-verb the bind hangs off the aggregate FQN ; the CHECK is its argument
  signal :reply       # returns an allow/deny verdict INLINE — and reply => in-process (the gate vetoes before the act)
end
